When your application encounters a "Connection refused" error, it means your microservice tried to initiate a handshake (TCP/SSL) with the database, but the database port either rejected the connection or was not listening at all.
This is different from a "Timeout" (where the connection started but stalled); this is an immediate "Go away" from the database server.
Here is the strategy to debug and resolve this, ranked from the most common to the most critical:

1. Database Connection Limits (The "Capacity" Wall)
This is the #1 culprit in Kubernetes environments. If your database's max_connections (e.g., in postgresql.conf) is set to 500, but your combined pool sizes across all your pods equal 600, the 501st connection attempt will be instantly refused.
• How to check: Run SHOW max_connections; and SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity; on your database.
• The Fix:
• Increase max_connections on the database server.
• Reduce your maximum-pool-size in your application properties to a more conservative number.
• Deploy a connection proxy (like PgBouncer). This allows thousands of app-side connections to "multiplex" into a smaller, stable pool of real DB connections.

Networking and Service Discovery (The "Path" Wall)
In Kubernetes, "Connection refused" often means the packet didn't even reach the database because of a network routing or service issue.
	• Check DNS: Is your connection string using a Service Name (e.g., db-service.namespace.svc.cluster.local) or an IP? Ensure the CoreDNS in Kubernetes can resolve that name.
	• Check Port: Verify the port the database is listening on versus the port in your application’s spring.datasource.url.
	• Check Network Policies: If you have strict NetworkPolicies in K8s, ensure your pod is explicitly allowed to talk to the DB pod or service on the DB port.
