3. Database "Panic" or Unavailability
If the database process is restarting, crashing, or stuck in a state where it can accept TCP connections but hasn't fully initialized its listening sockets, you will see this error.
• Check logs: Look at your database logs (not just app logs) for "Out of Memory" (OOM) kills or crashes during the time of the errors.
• StatefulSet Issues: If your DB is running as a StatefulSet in K8s, it might be in the middle of a rolling update where one node is down.
4. The "Thundering Herd" Recovery
If your database crashed and came back up, all your pods might be trying to reconnect at the exact same moment. This "thundering herd" of 50+ pods trying to create 20 connections each instantly hits the database's connection limit, causing a loop of "Connection refused."
	• The Fix: Implement Exponential Backoff in your application’s connection retry logic. Instead of retrying every 100ms, your pods should jitter their retry attempts (e.g., retry after 1s, then 2s, then 4s, etc.).


Immediate Action Plan
If you are currently facing this in production:
	1. Monitor the pg_stat_activity: Check how many connections are active right now. If it’s hitting your limit, you’ve found the smoking gun.
	2. Examine Pod Logs: Check if the errors occur during startup or randomly. If during startup, you likely have a configuration error; if random, you are hitting connection exhaustion under load.
	3. Proxy Strategy: If you cannot increase your database's max_connections, you must implement a connection proxy like PgBouncer. It is the industry standard for preventing "Connection refused" errors in microservice-to-database architectures.
• maximum-pool-size is set too high for the DB to handle.
• The connection-timeout is set too low (the threads are giving up before they can borrow a connection).
• minimum-idle is set too low (causing "cold start" latency).


Summary 
In a Kubernetes-based microservices architecture, this is usually a capacity or infrastructure issue. Here is the checklist to troubleshoot and resolve these errors.

1. Database & Resource Capacity (The "Limit" Check)
The most common cause is the database hitting its max_connections limit. If the database is at capacity, it will immediately reject new TCP handshake requests with Connection refused.
	• Check max_connections: Run SHOW max_connections; on your database.
	• Monitor Active Connections: Run SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity; during the time of failure. If the count is near the limit, you have found the bottleneck.
	• The Fix: * Increase the database max_connections setting.
		○ Crucially: Implement PgBouncer or a similar connection proxy to multiplex your application's many connections into a smaller, stable pool of database connections.

2. Connection Pool Configuration (The "App" Check)
Your microservices (likely using HikariCP) manage their own internal pools. If these are misconfigured, they can attempt to open more connections than your database infrastructure can support, especially when Kubernetes scales your pods up.
	• Audit Pool Sizes: Check spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size.
		○ Formula: (Max Pods) × (Max Pool Size per Pod) must be lower than your Database's max_connections.
	• Check Timeout Settings: Ensure your connection-timeout is realistic (e.g., 30s). If it is too short, your app will fail to even attempt the handshake under load.


